{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on the retirement-and-succession safeguards design at exact head 149ff9530296c06f5fbef1f12a3ce36669de18cb (origin/request/retirement-succession-safeguards, one commit over main 5ea68de4 adding notes/2026-09-02-retirement-succession-safeguards.md), artifact a81f1ed4, against governing request 0f6f785f and proposal 5545dfd0. The note does not authorize implementation (lines 12-13, 258). Satisfied: the lease-lapse rule (167-192) correctly relies on d8b2b7a6 removing the stale precondition while keeping the retired, promise and completion refusals; the chain analysis (139-144) is linear because every structural edge is sequence-increasing; the error texts (74-87) are stable; the proof obligations (231-252) are testable; it is filed as a proposal for hugh with an exact-head artifact and this independent review citing the request. Three blocking items. B1, positional activation has no mechanism (196-203): ProfileVersion is a checkpoint and cache gate, and the fold re-decides every event under one rule set on replay; decideSupersede at internal/workroom/fold.go:1049 has no sequence threshold. As written the lien would make the 502 legacy body-less withdrawals ineffective on replay and restore those requests, the opposite of line 203. The repository's precedent for tightening admission is prospective schema versioning (state@0/@1/@2, ratify@1, architecture.md 1070-1078): introduce workroom/supersede@1 carrying the body, apply the lien only to @1, and have internal/app refuse new @0 supersessions of requests; or specify an explicit ratified sequence boundary. Also state whether the guarded retire-if-unclaimed@0, which lowers to Supersede and routes through decideSupersede (fold.go 484-488, 1191-1196), is exempt via parsed.guardedRetirement, since lines 93-95 intend exemption but the enforcement site at line 67 would catch it. B2, second retirements (44, 102, 140): the lien applies only while the target is not already retired, yet main admits any number of live retirements of one target (retirementCauses, fold.go 2112-2135) and decideSupersede never checks prior retirement, so a second supersession carrying disposition=abandoned or a different body.successor bypasses the lien and \"one map from request to admitted successor\" is ill-defined. State that a supersession of an already-retired request refuses any body field with a stable error, that the detector reads only the earliest effective unretired retirement carrying a structural body, and what restoring the request (retiring the retirement) does to the edge; the legacy edge already drops on f.retired(supersession) at fold.go 1183. B3, the \"existing attention source\" does not exist for these rows (146-165, 249-250): statusview's terminal set (view.go 135) drops superseded, satisfied and withdrawn rows before the attention source (view.go 380-388); the UI files superseded, withdrawn and cancelled rows under the closed population (rows.ts 82), rowState returns the bare status word for closed rows regardless of attention (rows.ts 152-161), and needsAttention fires only on disputed or superseded-world (147-150). A broken row cannot reach Needs attention without changing the layer-6 terminal filter and the layer-7 population contract; the note assigns only layer 7 at line 250. Also decide which rows carry the qualifier: \"starting at a structurally replaced request\" (125) makes every request in an N-hop broken chain an attention row; qualify one row per chain and say which. Non-blocking: recovery when the reassigner is gone (89-102, 154-155): the replacement half must be signed by the guarded-retirement author (fold.go 1264-1266), so \"retry the same idempotency key\" is unavailable in the lease-lapse case the rule exists for; add the second route, the author or a ratifier supersedes the guarded retirement, which restores the request, then reassigns afresh. Rule 1 also depends on the stale-bases lane: a successor must directly rest on the target (51) and the audit's cases are stale requests, which refuseDeadBases refuses on main without the override and admits with body.stale_bases at b1caf459; lines 181-186 name d8b2b7a6 as prerequisite for rule 3 only, so say it for rule 1 or name the override. The same-requester rule (50) means a ratifier retiring a promised request whose requester has left can only abandon; acceptable, say so. Architecture: layer 5 fold and admission contract changes (supersede body, lien, successor map, qualifier); layer 6 statusview attention selection changes, undeclared (B3); layer 7 gs, MCP and UI (supersede body argument, batch, populations and rendering); layers 1-4 unchanged as claimed; architecture.md, supersede.md and reassign_if_unclaimed.md must move in the implementation head, the note says so for architecture only. Security: authority is unchanged (the author-or-ratifier branch of decideSupersede); a successor names an already-effective request by the same requester with a direct child edge, so no actor can bind another's request; a frontier race can only refuse; the detector is read-only over signed structure, carries ids and an enum and stays within existing caps; presence stays outside durable authority; edges are sequence-increasing so no cycles; legacy dot and comma-path artifacts are irrelevant to request succession. Residual risks are B1 (a naive implementation flips 502 historical decisions on replay) and B2 (a second retirement can rewrite the edge). Simplification: the body flag is already minimal; consider scoping the lien to requests carrying body.conditions so review and authorization requests skip the ceremony; qualify one row per chain; supersede@1 versioning is simpler than a profile-position mechanism. Any head change needs a successor artifact and fresh review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a81f1ed4277171adbfecf3f0e865f22986ee42cb","head":"149ff9530296c06f5fbef1f12a3ce36669de18cb","head_news_acknowledge